      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Given that $\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3$, we can rewrite the logarithms in terms of the base $x$ and base $3$, respectively.\n\nWe know that $\\log_x 4 = \\log_x (2^2) = 2\\log_x 2$.\n\nSimilarly, $\\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{3^3} 3 = \\log_{3^3} (3^1) = \\frac{\\log_3 3}{\\log_3 (3^3)} = \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, we have $2\\log_x 2 = \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nDividing both sides by 2, we get $\\log_x 2 = \\frac{1}{6}$.\n\nThis means that $x^{\\frac{1}{6}} = 2$.\n\nTo solve for $x$, we raise both sides to the 6th power: $(x^{\\frac{1}{6}})^6 = 2^6$.\n\nThis simplifies to $x = 64$.\n\nSo, the value of $x$ is $\\boxed{64}$.",
