    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start with the equation $\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3$. Using the change of base formula for logarithms, we can rewrite this as:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}\n\\]\nSince $27 = 3^3$, we have $\\log 27 = \\log (3^3) = 3\\log 3$. Substituting this in, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3\\log 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\\[\n3\\log 4 = \\log x\n\\]\nUsing the property of logarithms that $a\\log b = \\log b^a$, we can rewrite this as:\n\\[\n\\log 4^3 = \\log x \\implies \\log 64 = \\log x \\implies x = 64\n\\]\nTherefore, the solution is $\\boxed{64}$.",
