      "text": "\\begin{align*}\n\\log_{x} 4 &= \\log_{27} 3 \\\\\n\\log_{x} 4 &= \\log_{3^3} 3 \\\\\n4\\log_{x} (3^3) &= 3\\log_{3} (3) \\\\\n4\\log_{x} (3^3) &= 3\\log_{3} 3 \\\\\n4(3\\log_{x} 3) &= 3(1) \\\\\n12 \\log_{x} 3 &= 3 \\\\\n\\log_{x} 3 &= \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFrom the equation, we can get \\[4 \\log_{x} 3 = \\log_{x^{4}} 3^{4} = \\log_{x^{4}} 81 = \\log_{3^3} 81 = \\log_{3} 9 = 2.\\] Therefore, \\[\\log_{x} 3 = \\left(\\frac{\\log_{3} 3}{\\log_{3} x}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\log_{3} x} = \\frac{1}{4}.\\] This implies that \\[\\log_{3} x = 4.\\] So, \\[x = 3^{4} = \\boxed{81}.\\] Plugging this answer into the original equation to check, we see that the result holds true. The answer is 81",
